,c2,x0,x1,x2,x3,x4,x5]; diff = [x0,x1,x2,x3,x4,x5]; required = [x0,x4]; unresolved=[].\n- Initial test of full diff: FAIL (contains x0,x4). tests=1.\n- n=2: partitions: [x0,x1,x2] and [x3,x4,x5] (k=3). Test complement of first: [x3,x4,x5] -> contains x4 but not x0? Actually set {x3,x4,x5} contains x4 but not x0, so test returns PASS (not FAIL). Test complement of second: [x0,x1,x2] -> contains x0 but not x4, PASS. So no reduction, n=4 (since 2*2=4). n=4: partitions: [x0],[x1],[x2],[x3,x4,x5] (since len=6, 4 subsets: 2 full and 2 partial? Let's compute: k=6//4=1, remainder=2, so first two subsets size 2? Wait: Implementation: k = len//n, remainder = len%n. For len=6, n=4: k=1, remainder=2. So subsets: size 2 for first two, size 1 for next two? That would be: subset0: indices0-1 [x0,x1]; subset1: indices2-3 [x2,x3]; subset2: index4 [x4]; subset3: index5 [x5]. That's not evenly contiguous? Actually it's correct: we assign additional element to first remainder subsets. So subsets: [x0,x1], [x2,x3], [x4], [x5]. Test complements:\n  - complement of [x0,x1]: [x2,x3,x4,x5] contains x0? no, contains x4 only -> PASS.\n  - complement of [x2,x3]: [x0,x1,x4,x5] contains x0 and x4? Actually set {x0,x1,x4,x5} contains both x0 and x4 -> FAIL. So delta becomes complement: [x0,x1,x4,x5]. n=max(2, 4-1=3) but we reset n=2? Usually after reduction, n is set back to 2. The algorithm sets n = max(2, n-1). So n=3. Then we repeat with delta=[x0,x1,x4,x5]. n=3: partitions: len=4, n=3: k=1, rem=1. Subsets: size2,size1,size1 -> [x0,x1], [x4], [x5]. Test complements:\n    - complement of [x0,x1]: [x4,x5] contain x0? no, contains x4 -> PASS.\n    - complement of [x4]: [x0,x1,x5] contains x0 and x4? contains x0 but not x4 -> PASS.\n    - complement of [x5]: [x0,x1,x4] contains both x0 and x4 -> FAIL. So delta becomes [x0,x1,x4]. n=max(2,2)=2.\n  - Now delta=[x0,x1,x4], len=3, n=2: partitions: size2 and size1? k=1, rem=1 -> [x0,x1], [x4]. Test complements:\n    - complement of [x0,x1]: [x4] contains x0? no, PASS.\n    - complement of [x4]: [x0,x1] contains x0 but not x4 -> PASS.\n    So no reduction. n=min(4,3)=3. n=3: partitions: [x0,x1], [x4]? Actually len=3, n=3: subsets: [x0], [x1], [x4] (since k=1,rem=0). Test complements:\n       - complement of [x0]: [x1,x4] contains x4 but not x0 -> PASS\n       - complement of [x1]: [x0,x4] contains both -> FAIL. So delta becomes [x0,x4]. n=max(2,2)=2.\n    - delta=[x0,x4], len=2, n=2: partitions: [x0], [x4]. Test complements:\n       - complement of [x0]: [x4] contains x0? no, PASS.\n       - complement of [x4]: [x0] contains x4? no, PASS.\n      No reduction. n=min(4,2)=4 -> break since n>len? Actually n=4 > len=2, break. Return [x0,x4].\nSo isolated_difference = ['x0','x4'], tests: initial 1 + 2 (first round, two tests) + 3 (second round) + 2 (third round) + 3 (fourth round) + 2 (fifth round) = 1+2+3+2+3+2=13? Actually we need to count. Let's re-evaluate test count: initial 1. Then n=2: two tests (complements of two subsets) => total3. n=4: four tests (but in our implementation we break after first failure, so we only test until we find a failing complement. In the first n=4, we tested complement of subset0 and subset1; subset1 failed, so we stop after 2 tests. So total becomes 5. Then n=3 for new delta: three subsets, test complements until failure: first two pass, third fails => 3 tests, total 8. Then n=2: two tests, both pass => 2 tests, total 10. Then n=3: three tests, second fails => 3 tests, total 13. Then n=2: two tests, both pass => 2 tests, total 15. Then n=4>len, break. So 15 tests. Is that correct? Possibly. But we must verify if the algorithm should stop when n reaches length of delta? Our implementation will try n=4 only if no reduction in n=2, but we already reduced? Actually after reducing to [x0,x4], we go back to n=2, test both complements, no reduction, then n=min(2*2,2)=4, then we attempt to split into 4 subsets? len is 2, so when we compute subsets for n=4, we have k=0, remainder=2 -> first two subsets size1, next two size0? The loop will create subsets: for i in range(4): size = k + (1 if i<remainder else 0) -> for i=0: size=1; i=1: size=1; i=2: size=0; i=3: size=0. We skip size zero subsets. So we get two subsets: [x0] and [x4]. That's same as n=2. So we test complements again (2 tests), no reduction, then n=8? Actually after not found, we set n = min(2*n, len(delta)) = min(8,2)=2? That's weird. Classic algorithm says if no reduction and n < |delta|, double n, else break. So if n >= |delta|, break. Here n=4 > |delta|=2, so after failing to reduce with n=4, we break. But we already tested the same subsets as n=2? So we would do extra 2 tests unnecessarily. To avoid redundant tests, we should check if n >= len(delta) before starting the loop. The algorithm typically doubles n only while n < |delta|. I'll adjust: in the while loop, we set n = 2; then while len(delta) > 1: create subsets based on n; if n > len(delta): break? Actually standard: if n > len(delta), break (cannot partition further). Also after reduction, we reset n to 2. So better implementation:\n\ndef ddmin(delta):\n    n = 2\n    while len(delta) > 1:\n        # partition into at most n subsets, but not more than len(delta)\n        actual_n = min(n, len(delta))\n        subsets = []\n        k = len(delta) // actual_n\n        remainder = len(delta) % actual_n\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(actual_n):\n            size = k + (1 if i < remainder else 0)\n            subsets.append(delta[start:start+size])\n            start += size\n        found = False\n        for subset in subsets:\n            complement = [x for x in delta if x not in subset]  # maintain order\n            if test(complement) == 'FAIL':\n                delta = complement\n                n = 2\n                found = True\n                break\n        if not found:\n            if n >= len(delta):\n                break\n            n = min(2*n, len(delta))\n    return delta\n\nThis is cleaner.\n\nNow recalculate tests for first case with corrected algorithm.\n\n- Start: delta = [x0,x1,x2,x3,x4,x5]; test full: 1.\n- n=2, actual_n=2, subsets: [x0,x1,x2], [x3,x4,x5]. Test complements: first complement [x3,x4,x5] -> PASS (1 test), second complement [x0,x1,x2] -> PASS (2 tests). No reduction, so n = min(4,6)=4.\n- n=4, actual_n=4, subsets: [x0,x1], [x2,x3], [x4], [x5] (as computed). Test complements: first -> PASS (3), second -> FAIL (4). delta = [x0,x1,x4,x5], reset n=2.\n- delta len=4, n=2, actual_n=2, subsets: [x0,x1], [x4,x5]. Test complements: first -> PASS (5), second -> PASS (6). No reduction, n = min(4,4)=4.\n- n=4, actual_n=4, subsets: [x0],[x1],[x4],[x5]. Test complements: first -> PASS (7), second -> PASS (8), third -> PASS (9), fourth -> FAIL? Actually complement of [x5] is [x0,x1,x4] -> contains x0 and x4? Yes, contains both -> FAIL (10). delta = [x0,x1,x4], n=2.\n- delta len=3, n=2, actual_n=2, subsets: [x0,x1], [x4]? Wait k=3//2=1, rem=1 -> subsets: [x0], [x1,x4]? Actually careful: k=1, rem=1, so first subset size 2 (k+1), second subset size 1. So subsets: [x0,x1], [x4]. That's correct. Test complements: [x4] -> PASS (11), [x0,x1] -> PASS (12). No reduction, n = min(4,3)=3.\n- n=3, actual_n=3, subsets: k=1, rem=0 -> [x0], [x1], [x4]. Test complements: [x1,x4] -> PASS (13), [x0,x4] -> FAIL (14). delta = [x0,x4], n=2.\n- delta len=2, n=2, actual_n=2, subsets: [x0], [x4]. Test complements: [x4] -> PASS (15), [x0] -> PASS (16). No reduction, n = min(4,2)=4.\n- n=4, actual_n=2 (since min(4,2)=2) -> same subsets again. Test complements again (17 and 18), no reduction, now n >= len(delta) -> break. So total tests = 18.\n\nBut we could have optimized by checking if n > len(delta) and not test again. So we should break when n > len(delta) before testing. In the step n=4, len=2, we set actual_n=2, but n > len, we should break before testing. So modify: after not found, if n >= len(delta): break. Actually after the test loop, if not found, we then check n >= len(delta). But before that, we already tested with actual_n=2. If n=4 and len=2, we still test because actual_n=2. That's redundant. Better to check before partitioning: if n > len(delta): break. So in the while loop, after resetting n to 2, before partition, we can check if n > len? Not needed. But in the case where n becomes larger than len, we should break. So modify:\n\nif not found:\n    if n >= len(delta):\n        break\n    n = min(2*n, len(delta))\n\nIn our step, n=4, len=2, so n >= len, break. So we skip the redundant test. So after 16 tests, we break. So tests = 16.\n\nThis seems plausible.\n\nNow, we need to ensure that the algorithm matches the expected test count from the fixture? The fixture doesn't give test counts, so we are free to implement any correct ddmin. But the task says \"Preserve the registered PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED oracle semantics.\" The oracle semantics are defined by the case's required_difference and unresolved_difference. So our oracle is correct. The number of tests is cumulative from the algorithm.\n\nWe should also consider that the full set may contain duplicates? The difference list may have duplicates? Unlikely.\n\nNow, implement solve as described.\n\nOne more nuance: The \"isolated_difference\" should be the minimal difference in the order they appear in failing. Our algorithm preserves order because we always maintain order through complements (removing elements while preserving order). The result will be a sublist of the original diff in the same order.\n\nEdge case: if required_difference is empty? Then any subset would pass? But the problem likely expects required_difference non-empty. If empty, the full set passes, then we cannot isolate. But we can handle: if full set test returns PASS, then isolated_difference is [].\n\nNow, write the code.\n\nWe'll include docstring maybe.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON.\n\nMake sure to escape the Python source properly for JSON.\n\nLet's write the Python function.\n\nWe'll use standard library only.\n\nWe'll also ensure no mutation of case.\n\nWe'll define test as a closure with nonlocal counter.\n\nWe'll use list comprehension for complement to maintain order.\n\nImplementation details:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    passing = case['passing']\n    failing = case['failing']\n    required = case['required_difference']\n    unresolved = case.get('unresolved_difference', [])\n    \n    pass_set = set(passing)\n    diff = []\n    for item in failing:\n        if item not in pass_set:\n            diff.append(item)\n    \n    tests = [0]\n    \n    def test(subset):\n        tests[0] += 1\n        sset = set(subset)\n        # Check required difference: all must be present to fail\n        if all(r in sset for r in required):\n            return 'FAIL'\n        # Check unresolved: if any unresolved element present, unresolved\n        for u in unresolved:\n            if u in sset:\n                return 'UNRESOLVED'\n        return 'PASS'\n    \n    # Initial test of full set\n    if test(diff) != 'FAIL':\n        # If full set does not fail, return empty\n        return {'isolated_difference': [], 'tests': tests[0]}\n    \n    # Delta debugging (ddmin)\n    def ddmin(delta):\n        n = 2\n        while len(delta) > 1:\n            # Determine actual number of partitions (not exceeding length)\n            actual_n = min(n, len(delta))\n            # Partition delta into actual_n consecutive chunks\n            k = len(delta) // actual_n\n            r = len(delta) % actual_n\n            subsets = []\n            start = 0\n            for i in range(actual_n):\n                size = k + (1 if i < r else 0)\n                subsets.append(delta[start:start+size])\n                start += size\n            found = False\n            for subset in subsets:\n                # Complement is delta with elements of subset removed, preserving order\n                complement = [x for x in delta if x not in subset]\n                if test(complement) == 'FAIL':\n                    delta = complement\n                    n = 2\n                    found = True\n                    break\n            if not found:\n                if n >= len(delta):\n                    break\n                n = min(2 * n, len(delta))\n        return delta\n    \n    isolated = ddmin(diff)\n    return {'isolated_difference': isolated, 'tests': tests[0]}\n\nNow, we need to embed this as a JSON string.\n\nWe'll produce the final output.\n\nPotential improvements: Handle empty diff case: if diff is empty, then no difference, isolated_difference [] and tests 1? Actually if diff empty, then the test of empty set should fail? required_difference may be non-empty but cannot be in empty set, so test returns PASS. So we return empty. That's fine.\n\nNow, finalize.\n\nLet's write the JSON envelope: {\"implementation\": \"...\"} with escaped Python code.\n\nWe'll ensure the code is properly escaped.\n\nI'll write the string."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1167,"completion_tokens":7645,"total_tokens":8812,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":7186},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":15},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}